{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789383693,"nonce":"qvhUYurYgiRfQSCHrpdDoktBbFs=","title":"A typed callback held its Wren function by value, past the Wren cycle","message":"A typed callback (`callback::function_for_typed`, since f9d5cac) kept the Wren function as a bare `Value` in the `Callback` object and marked it from the trace hook. That keeps it through the core's collection, but not through a Wren cycle: the cycle lets go of every object Wren's own marking did not reach unless the object has a shadow, and only then waits for the core's mark. A `Fn` returned to Haxe has no Wren reference and no shadow, so the cycle freed it while the Haxe closure still called it. The var-args form held a ref (a shadow) and was safe.\n\nSeen as the swarm arena crashing in `wren_get_upvalue` (the tween closure) at 1000 boids × 600 frames × 3 runs once `onCollide` stopped boxing its result: fewer allocations moved the cycle onto the closure's lifetime. Under lldb it did not reproduce.\n\nFix: the callback holds the function's ref, `wrenref::wrap_foreign`, rooted across the callback's allocation; the call goes to the function itself through `unwrap_foreign`, so the extra hop costs one field read (H→W closure cell 37 → 38 ns). `behind` unwraps the ref so a closure going back to Wren is the same function. Test: `haxe_imports` keeps a typed closure in a Haxe static across both collectors and calls it after.","files":null}]}